# `core::primitives::char` -- `char`'s declaration site.
#
# A Unicode scalar value: a 4-byte decoded codepoint, never a byte and never a
# UTF-16 code unit. Its domain is `0..=0x10FFFF` with the surrogate block
# `0xD800..=0xDFFF` excluded -- so unlike every other built-in, `char` carries
# a **validity invariant**, and not every bit pattern of its width is a value.
#
# That invariant is why the casts are asymmetric. Every `char` converts *out*
# to an integer (`<i32>c`, `<u32>c`), because every scalar value is a valid
# integer. Converting *in* is allowed only from `u8`, whose every value is a
# valid scalar value; `<char>some_u32` is refused, because most `u32`s are not
# codepoints and there is no checked conversion to offer instead yet.
#
# It is also why arithmetic on a `char` yields `u32` and never comes back:
# `c + 1` is a number, not a character, and no implicit path leads from that
# number back to a `char` where it could smuggle in a surrogate.
#
# The block is empty because everything `char` wants is currently blocked on
# that missing conversion -- `from_u32`, a `chars()` decoder, `Successor` (and
# so ranges), case mapping. Inspecting methods (`is_ascii`, `is_digit`,
# `len_utf8`) need nothing new and belong here whenever someone writes them.
# See `docs/01-primitives.md`.
primitive char { }
